# Plane-host cookie bounce — GitLab OAuth consent (and other cookie-only Coder pages) when middle is on another host

**Status:** validated end to end. This document is self-contained: problem, design, security model,
all code (middle, Lambda, portal), Coder/GitLab configuration and AWS resources.

- Bounce mechanics validated on **real AWS** (ALB + Lambda in VPC + IAM/STS, public domain, HTTPS)
  and locally (an ALB emulator running the same handler).
- **GitLab OAuth consent validated with a real gitlab.com OAuth application**: portal → plane host →
  GitLab → Coder → back to the portal. At the end the consent session is **revoked and its cookie
  cleared**, so the user cannot reuse it to browse the Coder dashboard.

---

## 1. Problem

The portal talks to **middle** (`middle.example.com`); middle talks to one or more Coder control
planes (`coder-a.example.com`, …) with per-user Coder API tokens. Most things work with tokens in
API calls, but some Coder pages authenticate **only with the `coder_session_token` cookie on the
plane host**:

| Page | Why a token in the URL/API doesn't work |
| --- | --- |
| External-auth consent `/external-auth/{id}` and callback `/external-auth/{id}/callback` (GitLab OAuth) | GitLab redirects the browser back to the callback; only cookies travel on that redirect. Without the cookie Coder answers `303 /login`. Coder also requires the user to finish the flow on the same domain they started it on. |
| Built-in web terminal `/@user/ws.agent/terminal` | Dashboard SPA; its fetch/WebSocket calls use the cookie only. Not needed when ttyd is used as a workspace app. |

Constraints:

- A browser only accepts a **host-only** cookie (no `Domain` attribute) from a response **served by
  that exact host** (port and path don't matter). Middle is on another host, so it cannot set it.
- A `Domain=example.com` cookie set from a parent domain is ruled out: the browser would send the
  user's Coder token to **every workspace app subdomain** (`*.coder-a.example.com`), i.e. to code
  running in workspaces.
- Coder's `?coder_session_token=` query parameter works for workspace apps but not for dashboard
  pages or the OAuth callback.

## 2. Solution overview

Put a tiny, stateless **bounce** on the plane host with **one ALB listener rule**
(`host = coder-a.example.com AND path = /_portal/*` → Lambda). Middle stays where it is and keeps
all the logic. The Lambda proves who it is to middle with **IAM** (a pre-signed
`sts:GetCallerIdentity`), so there is no shared secret.

Components:

| Component | Role |
| --- | --- |
| Portal (browser SPA) | Shows GitLab link status; "Connect GitLab" asks middle for a one-time code and form-POSTs it to the plane host |
| Middle | Issues one-time codes (user-authenticated), verifies the Lambda's IAM identity, mints/revokes Coder tokens, returns redirect targets |
| ALB rule on the plane host | Sends `/_portal/*` to the bounce Lambda; everything else to Coder as before |
| Bounce Lambda (one IAM role per plane) | Redeems the code with middle, sets the host-only cookie, redirects; at `/_portal/done` revokes + clears the cookie and returns to the portal |
| Coder | Normal external auth (GitLab) configuration; unchanged |

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant P as Portal (browser)
  participant M as Middle
  participant A as ALB coder-a.example.com
  participant L as Bounce Lambda (role per plane)
  participant S as AWS STS
  participant C as Coder plane A
  participant G as GitLab
  P->>M: GET /v1/external-auth/primary-gitlab → authenticated: false
  P->>M: POST /v1/bounce/start {plane, purpose: external-auth, provider} (user JWT)
  M-->>P: {bounce_url, code} — one-time, 60s, bound to user+plane+redirect
  P->>A: form POST code → https://coder-a.example.com/_portal/bounce (same tab)
  A->>L: rule host=coder-a + path=/_portal/* (priority before Coder)
  L->>M: POST /internal/bounce/redeem {code, host, iam: signed GetCallerIdentity}
  M->>S: replay signed request (fixed endpoint)
  S-->>M: arn …:assumed-role/coder-bounce-a/…
  M->>M: role→plane allowlist, consume code, check host, mint 5-min "bounce-a" token
  M-->>L: {session_token, redirect_path, max_age: 300}
  L-->>P: 302 /external-auth/primary-gitlab/callback?redirect=/_portal/done + Set-Cookie (host-only)
  P->>C: callback (cookie) → Coder stores oauth_redirect=/_portal/done
  C-->>P: 307 gitlab.com/oauth/authorize (PKCE + state)
  P->>G: sign in if needed, consent
  G-->>P: 302 back to Coder callback ?code=…
  P->>C: callback (cookie) → Coder exchanges code, stores the user's GitLab link
  C-->>P: 307 /_portal/done
  P->>A: GET /_portal/done (cookie)
  A->>L: same rule
  L->>M: POST /internal/bounce/done {host, session_token, iam}
  M->>C: delete that API key (only if it is the "bounce-a" key)
  M-->>L: {redirect_url: portal}
  L-->>P: 302 portal/?linked=1 + Set-Cookie coder_session_token=; Max-Age=0
  P->>M: GET /v1/external-auth/primary-gitlab → authenticated: true
```

## 3. Flow, step by step (HTTP level)

1. **Status.** Portal → middle `GET /v1/external-auth/primary-gitlab?plane=a` (IdP JWT). Middle calls
   Coder `GET /api/v2/external-auth/primary-gitlab` **as the user** (with the user's main token, server
   side) and returns `{authenticated, display_name, login}`. `false` → show "Connect GitLab";
   `true` → "GitLab: linked".
2. **Start.** User clicks "Connect GitLab". Portal → middle `POST /v1/bounce/start`
   `{"plane":"a","purpose":"external-auth","provider":"primary-gitlab"}`. Middle computes the redirect
   itself (`/external-auth/primary-gitlab/callback?redirect=%2F_portal%2Fdone`), stores a random
   one-time code `{email, plane, redirect, purpose, exp: now+60s}` and returns
   `{"bounce_url":"https://coder-a.example.com/_portal/bounce","code":"…","method":"POST"}`.
3. **Bounce.** Portal builds a hidden form and submits it **in the same tab**:
   `POST https://coder-a.example.com/_portal/bounce` with body `code=…`. The ALB rule sends it to the Lambda.
4. **Redeem.** Lambda signs (does not send) an `sts:GetCallerIdentity` request with its execution
   role, including two signed headers: `X-Middle-Server-Id: middle-prod` and
   `X-Middle-Code-Sha256: sha256("redeem\n" + code)`. It POSTs
   `{code, host, iam:{headers, body}}` to middle `/internal/bounce/redeem`.
5. **Verify + mint.** Middle checks the audience header and the hash, requires both headers to be
   in `SignedHeaders`, requires the body to be exactly `Action=GetCallerIdentity&Version=2011-06-15`,
   replays the request to its **configured** STS endpoint, parses the returned ARN
   (`arn:aws:sts::<acct>:assumed-role/<role>/<session>`), maps account + role → plane via the
   allowlist, consumes the code (single use), checks code plane == caller plane and host == plane
   host, then mints a **dedicated 5-minute Coder key `bounce-a`** for the user and returns
   `{session_token, redirect_path, max_age: 300}`.
6. **Cookie + redirect.** Lambda answers `302 Location: /external-auth/primary-gitlab/callback?redirect=%2F_portal%2Fdone`
   with `Set-Cookie: coder_session_token=…; Path=/; Max-Age=300; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax`
   (no `Domain`), `Cache-Control: no-store`, `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`.
7. **Coder → GitLab.** The callback without `?code` makes Coder start the provider redirect
   immediately (skips Coder's "Login with GitLab" page). Coder stores the `redirect` value in its
   `oauth_redirect` cookie, sets `oauth_state` / PKCE cookies and answers
   `307 https://gitlab.com/oauth/authorize?...&scope=write_repository+read_api&code_challenge_method=S256`.
8. **Consent.** The user signs in to GitLab if needed and approves (GitLab auto-approves an app the
   user already authorized). GitLab redirects to Coder's callback with `?code=…&state=…`; the
   cookie is sent (top-level navigation, `SameSite=Lax`), Coder exchanges the code and stores the
   user's GitLab token, then redirects to `oauth_redirect` = `/_portal/done`.
9. **Done.** `GET /_portal/done` hits the Lambda (same rule). It reads `coder_session_token` from the
   `Cookie` header, signs a proof bound to `sha256("done\n" + token)` and POSTs
   `{host, session_token, iam}` to middle `/internal/bounce/done`. Middle verifies the caller the
   same way, resolves the user from the token, deletes the API key whose id is the token's prefix
   **only if its name is `bounce-a`**, and returns `{redirect_url: "https://portal.example.com/"}`.
10. **Back to the portal.** Lambda answers `302 https://portal.example.com/?linked=1` with
    `Set-Cookie: coder_session_token=; Path=/; Max-Age=0; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax`. The portal
    strips `?linked=1` and refreshes the status → "GitLab: linked". Visiting `https://coder-a.example.com/`
    now shows Coder's login page.
11. **Git in workspaces.** Coder injects the user's GitLab token via `GIT_ASKPASS`, so HTTPS
    `git clone` / `push` to GitLab works inside workspaces whose template declares the provider.

## 4. Security model

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| How does middle know **which user**? | From the **code**, minted while the user was authenticated to middle (IdP JWT). The Lambda never asserts identity. |
| How does middle know the caller is **the bounce Lambda**? | The Lambda sends a **pre-signed `sts:GetCallerIdentity`**; middle replays it to a **fixed** STS endpoint and gets the caller's ARN. No shared secret; middle needs no AWS credentials. `GetCallerIdentity` needs no IAM permission, and a forged signature is rejected by STS. (Same technique as HashiCorp Vault's AWS "iam" auth method, whose `X-Vault-AWS-IAM-Server-ID` header plays the role of our `X-Middle-Server-Id`.) |
| Why can't plane A's Lambda redeem plane B's codes? | The allowlist maps **role → plane**; the code's plane must equal the caller's plane and the request host must equal that plane's host. |
| Why can't a captured signature be reused? | `X-Middle-Server-Id` (audience: this middle) and `X-Middle-Code-Sha256` = sha256(`action` + `\n` + value) are signed: a proof is valid for one middle, one action (`redeem` or `done`), one value (code or session token). SigV4 signatures expire (~15 min); codes are single-use, 60 s. |
| What if the code leaks? | It contains no token, is single-use and short-lived, bound to one plane host, and only the trusted Lambda can redeem it. It travels in a form POST body, so it is not in history, `Referer` or ALB access logs. |
| Open redirect? | Redirect paths are computed by middle; the Lambda only accepts a same-origin path (`/…`, not `//…`) on redeem, and on `/done` only an `http(s)` URL returned by middle (`bounce.done_redirect`). |
| Can the consent cookie be used to browse the Coder dashboard? | Only during the consent round trip. It carries a **dedicated 5-minute key (`bounce-<plane>`)**, which `/done` revokes; the cookie itself is cleared. `/internal/bounce/done` only ever deletes keys named `bounce-<plane>`, never the user's main `middle-<plane>` key. |
| What if the user abandons consent mid-way? | The bounce key and cookie expire on their own after 5 minutes. |
| Workspace app subdomains? | The cookie is host-only on `coder-a.example.com`; it is never sent to `*.coder-a.example.com`. The ALB rule matches the exact plane host only. |

Allowlist matching: STS returns `arn:aws:sts::<acct>:assumed-role/<role-name>/<session>` — the role
**path is not included**, so match **account + role name**; optionally pin the role's unique id
(`AROA…`, the prefix of `UserId` before `:`) so a deleted and recreated role with the same name is
not trusted automatically.

### 4.1 Tokens and timers

| Token | Where it lives | Lifetime | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Main `middle-<plane>` key | **Middle only**; never sent to the browser for consent | 24 h, re-minted | Link status, unlink, workspaces… — server side |
| Dedicated `bounce-<plane>` key | The consent cookie on the plane host | 5 min, **revoked at `/_portal/done`** | GitLab consent round trip only |

- The bounce key is minted **at redeem** (when the Lambda redeems the code), not when the user clicks;
  minting first deletes any previous `bounce-<plane>` key, so there is at most one per user.
- Naming: the bounce key is `bounce-a`, not `middle-a-bounce` — middle treats `middle-a-*` names as
  legacy variants of its main key and deletes them when re-minting.

| Timer | Covers | Recommended | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| One-time code (`code_ttl_seconds`) | Click → form POST to `/_portal/bounce` (sub-second) | 30–60 s | Minted on click, single use, consumed **before** the GitLab page |
| Consent key + cookie (300 s today) | Bounce → GitLab (sign-in, 2FA/SSO, consent) → `/_portal/done` | 5–10 min | Too short and GitLab's redirect back finds no valid cookie → Coder login page, user restarts from the portal. Longer is low-risk: revoked at `/done`, expires if abandoned |

## 5. What was proven

### 5.1 Bounce mechanics

| Check | Local lab (ALB emulator) | AWS lab (real ALB + Lambda) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/_portal/*` on plane host → bounce, everything else → Coder | Emulator routing | ALB listener rule (priority 10) → Lambda target group |
| Lambda identity proven to middle | Assumed role via a lab IAM user | **Real Lambda execution role** (`assumed-role/coder-bounce-plane-a/coder-bounce-plane-a`) verified with STS |
| 302 + host-only cookie, no `Domain=` | `HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax` (http) | `HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax` |
| Cookie alone authenticates on the plane host | `/api/v2/users/me` = alice (401 without cookie) | same |
| No token/code in URLs | Form POST; clean address bar | same |
| Replay same code | 403 | 403 |
| Wrong audience (server id) | 403 `audience mismatch` | 403 |
| Caller not the trusted role (plain IAM user) | 403 `caller is not an assumed role` | — |
| Direct call to middle redeem without Lambda | — | 403 |

On AWS the ALB turns the Lambda's relative `Location` into an absolute URL on the same host (harmless).

### 5.2 GitLab external auth (real gitlab.com OAuth app)

| Check | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Portal shows link status | `authenticated: false` → "Connect GitLab"; `true` → "GitLab: linked" |
| Bounce → `/external-auth/primary-gitlab/callback?redirect=%2F_portal%2Fdone` | Coder sets `oauth_redirect=/_portal/done` and 307s to `gitlab.com/oauth/authorize` (PKCE S256, `scope=write_repository read_api`) |
| GitLab consent → Coder callback with the bounced cookie | Link stored |
| Coder → `/_portal/done` → portal | Browser lands on the portal, button shows "GitLab: linked" |
| Consent session after `/done` | Bounce key revoked (`revoked=true`, old token → 401), cookie cleared; Coder URL shows the login page, **not the dashboard** |
| Git inside the workspace | HTTPS `git clone` / push via Coder's GitLab credentials works |
| Unlink and re-link | Works; to see GitLab's consent screen again, revoke the app in GitLab → User Settings → Applications → Authorized applications |

## 6. Implementation

### 6.1 GitLab and Coder

**GitLab OAuth application** (user, group or instance level → Applications), one per plane host:

- Redirect URI: `https://coder-a.example.com/external-auth/primary-gitlab/callback` — the path segment
  must equal `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID`.
- Confidential: yes. Scopes: `write_repository`, `read_api` (Coder's GitLab defaults; requesting a
  scope the app doesn't have fails with "The requested scope is invalid, unknown, or malformed").

**Coder server environment** (per plane):

```bash
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID=primary-gitlab
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_TYPE=gitlab
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_CLIENT_ID=<GitLab "Application ID">
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_CLIENT_SECRET=<GitLab secret>

# Self-managed GitLab only:
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_AUTH_URL=https://gitlab.example.com/oauth/authorize
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_TOKEN_URL=https://gitlab.example.com/oauth/token
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_VALIDATE_URL=https://gitlab.example.com/oauth/token/info
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REVOKE_URL=https://gitlab.example.com/oauth/revoke
CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REGEX=gitlab\.example\.com
```

The callback URL is built from Coder's access URL (`CODER_ACCESS_URL=https://coder-a.example.com`).
More than one external-auth provider on a deployment requires a Coder Premium license; one GitLab
provider does not.

**Template** (declares the provider so workspaces get the token via `GIT_ASKPASS`):

```hcl
data "coder_external_auth" "gitlab" {
  id       = "primary-gitlab"
  optional = true   # builds don't block on it; the portal drives consent
}
```

**Coder API calls middle uses** (all with a user's token unless noted):

| Call | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /api/v2/external-auth/{id}` | Link status → `authenticated`, `display_name`, `user.login` |
| `DELETE /api/v2/external-auth/{id}` | Unlink the calling user |
| `GET /api/v2/users/me` | Resolve the user behind a token (at `/done`) |
| `GET /api/v2/users/{id}/keys/tokens` (owner token) | List the user's API keys (name, id) |
| `POST /api/v2/users/{id}/keys/tokens` (owner token) | Mint a key: `{"token_name":"bounce-a","lifetime":<nanoseconds>}` |
| `DELETE /api/v2/users/{id}/keys/{keyid}` (owner token) | Revoke a key. A Coder token is `<keyid>-<secret>`, so the key id is the token's prefix |

### 6.2 Middle

#### Configuration

```yaml
